      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Attached printed page 3 gives the constant-matrix eigenvector criterion and applies it to the flux Jacobian after (1.9). Printed pages 1 and 4 supply the real state-space and smoothness context.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation concerns a real flux derivative and its complete real eigenvector family.",
      "reasoning": "The selected passage and necessary surrounding context are visible in the authoritative source renderings. The supplied hashes identify the evidence; I have not independently recomputed them."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 1 specifies real m-component states and a real m-by-m matrix; printed page 3 substitutes the flux Jacobian.",
      "translation_evidence": "The binders use V = R^m, f : V → V, a state x in V, and a continuous real-linear derivative A.",
      "reasoning": "The principal scalar field, dimensions, and derivative type agree. The translated state named x is not the source's spatial coordinate x."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The sentence after (1.9) applies the criterion to f′(q), without specifying a state domain or an explicit global quantifier.",
      "translation_evidence": "The equivalence is at one fixed state, with eigenvalue and eigenvector witnesses allowed to depend on that state.",
      "reasoning": "Witness dependence matches a pointwise reading. Whether this exhausts the source's assertion that the equation is hyperbolic, rather than supplying its pointwise ingredient, remains unresolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The quasilinear expression uses an existing Jacobian. Printed page 4 qualifies passage to differential form by sufficient smoothness.",
      "translation_evidence": "The sole analytic hypothesis is that f has Fréchet derivative A at the chosen state.",
      "reasoning": "This appropriately makes derivative existence explicit for a pointwise spectral criterion. The translation does not assert a chain rule for nonsmooth solutions or a differential-integral equivalence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The selected conclusion calls the conservation-law equation hyperbolic when its Jacobian satisfies the preceding matrix criterion.",
      "translation_evidence": "The conclusion equates an existential derivative-matrix eigenbasis property with independent eigenvectors of the specified derivative. The translation explicitly reports no PDE or assertion about solutions.",
      "reasoning": "The spectral content is complete. Its identification with hyperbolicity of the conservation law is not expressed independently in the translated proposition. A definitional abstraction may suffice, but that identification cannot be inferred solely from a predicate or flux name."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source uses the flux Jacobian, real eigenvalues, and a basis providing unique real expansions.",
      "translation_evidence": "D001, D004, D018, D021, D056, D068, and D069 explain derivative existence, real eigenbases, independence, standard-coordinate conversion, matrix action, and bases.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied dependency interpretations preserve the relevant operators. D002–D082 account for the remaining structural interfaces without adding substantive conditions. This assesses the supplied ledger, without inspecting forbidden declarations."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The opening paragraph presents the spectral condition as the meaning of constant-system hyperbolicity; the sentence after (1.9) references that condition using an if statement.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation gives a biconditional between two presentations of the same derivative spectral property.",
      "reasoning": "The biconditional itself is a harmless definitional reformulation: uniqueness identifies B with A, and m independent vectors in R^m form a basis. It does not establish a separate converse concerning PDE solutions."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.9) tests f′(q), not the flux value f(q).",
      "translation_evidence": "A is a derivative of f at the state; the existential derivative B has the same derivative property and hence equals A.",
      "reasoning": "The flux, state, derivative operator, and derivative matrix retain distinct roles. Standard-coordinate conversion preserves the operator action."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The criterion requires m corresponding independent eigenvectors in R^m.",
      "translation_evidence": "Fin m indexes both coordinates and exactly m eigenpairs, with A(v_p) = λ_p v_p.",
      "reasoning": "Zero-based indexing changes no mathematical content. Eigenvalues may depend on the state and no unintended coefficient or ordering is introduced."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The passage specifies no admissible state set and no formal lower bound on m.",
      "translation_evidence": "The flux is defined on all of R^m, differentiation is ambient, and m may be zero.",
      "reasoning": "The empty-dimensional extension is harmless for the spectral identity. The source does not settle whether an everywhere-defined flux adequately represents all intended admissible-state domains; domain-wide applicability requires adjudication."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source requires independent eigenvectors but does not require distinct, positive, or nonzero eigenvalues.",
      "translation_evidence": "Repeated and zero eigenvalues are allowed; no ordering, invertibility, or orthogonality is required. Empty families are allowed when m = 0.",
      "reasoning": "The meaningful spectral boundary cases are preserved. The zero-dimensional extension uses the ordinary empty basis and removes no positive-dimensional cases."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 4 includes linear fluxes f(q) = Aq.",
      "translation_evidence": "A differentiable linear flux supplies the derivative hypothesis; the spectral condition still requires a complete eigenbasis.",
      "reasoning": "Positive-dimensional examples satisfy the hypothesis. A real Jordan block without a complete eigenbasis makes both spectral sides false, so the predicate has substantive content despite the equivalence being definitional."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected result is a spectral hyperbolicity criterion, not a discretization, recurrence, or numerical algorithm.",
